; P02 SCRATCH/LIVE STATE DIRECTORY -- STATIC-DECODE, PURPOSE AS INDICATED
; Absolute $0000-$1FFF under DBR=$03 accesses the low WRAM mirror; canonical
; WRAM addresses are $7E:xxxx. DP references assume the inherited DP=$0000.
; $0848-$0849: keyboard cursor VRAM address; initialized $5245.
; $084A-$084B: input cursor VRAM address; initialized $5146.
; $084C-$084D: tile readback/keyboard cursor rendering scratch.
; $084E-$0882: input glyph buffer (53 copied bytes); cursor permits index $35
; ($0883) as well. Copy/count asymmetry requires runtime edge-case testing.
; $0883-$0884: submitted/generated two-glyph prefix.
; $0885: submitted length glyph / temporary encoded symbol count.
; $0886-$0895: 16 packed live-state bytes before compression/after expansion.
; $0896-$0898: three little-endian additive checksum bytes.
; $08B8: input cursor index; clamped to $00-$35 by cursor movement.
; $08B9-$08BB: 24-bit zero bitmap, most significant byte first.
; $08BC onward: packed nonzero bytes; later reused as decode bitstream storage.
; $08EB: keyboard selection index ($00-$2A).
; $08EC: error/confirmation phase; high bit marks refresh pending.
; DP $00-$0F: codec/cursor/status scratch; DO NOT use as persistent save state.
; $0670 + index in $066F: rendering command queue used by password UI;
; $07B1 onward: temporary render script. Renderer extent/ownership UNKNOWN.
;
; P03 EXACT NORMAL PAYLOAD -- STATIC-DECODE
; Let P[0..15] be $0886-$0895. Decoder dispatch $D00B-$D059 calls 26 leaves
; at $D05A-$D14D. Encoder dispatch $D689-$D6E3 calls inverse leaves at
; $D6E4-$D7DD. Both use 8-bit accumulator loads/stores.
; All listed fields are exact storage mappings; P11 names observed UI consumers.
; Byte   Packed expression on generation                  Restore operation
;  00    ($10F1 << 4) | $10D0                             $10F1=P>>4;
;                                                        $10D0=P & $0F
;  01    $1084                                           $1084=P
;  02    $1085                                           $1085=P
;  03    $1086                                           $1086=P
;  04    $1087                                           $1087=P
;  05    $1088                                           $1088=P
;  06    $1089                                           $1089=P
;  07    (($10E0 << 2)&$04) | ($10E5&$FB)                 $10E0=(P>>2)&1;
;                                                        $10E5=P&$FB
;  08    (($10E1 << 4)&$F0) | ($10E3&$0F)                 high/low nibbles
;  09    (($10E2 & 1)<<7) | ($10EC&$7F)                   $10E2=P>>7;
;                                                        $10EC=P&$7F
;  0A    (($10E4 << 4)&$F0) | ($10E6&$0F)                 high/low nibbles
;  0B    (($10E7 << 4)&$F0) | ($10E9&$0F)                 high/low nibbles
;  0C    (($10E8 & 3)<<6) | ($10EF&$3F)                   high 2/low 6 bits
;  0D    (($10EA << 4)&$F0) | ($10EB&$0F)                 high/low nibbles
;  0E    ($10ED << 3) | ($10EE&7)                         $10ED=P>>3;
;                                                        $10EE=P&7
;  0F    ($10F0&$0F) | (($1800&1)<<4)                     $10F0=P&$0F;
;                                                        $1800 |= (P>>4)&1
; All generated expressions truncate to eight bits. $10D0 is NOT masked
; before OR: out-of-range values can contaminate $10F1's packed nibble.
; Native ROR/ROL in the $10E2/$10E8 leaves implements these bit extracts;
; final masks remove incoming carry dependence from the retained bits.
; $1800 restore uses TSB, not STA: loading a zero flag does NOT clear a one
; already in memory. Determine initial clearing before reusing this routine.
; $10F2-$10F3 (medals; preset low byte $64) and all other unlisted state are
; omitted from this payload.
; Omission from the payload does not prove reset/loss: later code may derive it.
;
; P04 CHECKSUM, ZERO REMOVAL, AND BITSTREAM -- STATIC-DECODE
; $CE54-$CE77: start 24-bit sum at zero; add each of 16 bytes at $0886 using
; ADC carry propagation into DP $00/$01/$02. Sum range $000000-$000FF0.
; Thus third stored checksum byte is always zero for native generated state.
; This is an unweighted sum, not encryption or a positional checksum. Swapping
; payload bytes or compensating changes can preserve it; it proves no legality.
; $D7DE-$D824: scan 19 bytes (payload plus checksum). For byte i, set bitmap
; bit (23-i) if it is zero; otherwise append it to $08BC onward. Bitmap's low
; five bits remain zero. Serialized bytes = bitmap[3] followed by nonzero bytes.
; $D825-$D895: consume that serialized stream MSB first in five-bit groups;
; final group is padded on the right with zeros. N=ceil((3+nonzero_count)*8/5).
; Native payload/checksum can generate at most 18 nonzero bytes: N<=34.
; An all-zero synthetic payload yields N=5, but cannot be loaded (see below).
; $D896-$D9AE maps each group v to glyph(v | $40); length is glyph(N+$3B).
; On load $D14E-$D1A0 clears $08B9-$08EB inclusive, normalizes length, subtracts
; $3B and rejects a negative result. It normalizes N+1 bytes at $0886 onward
; (one extra beyond the symbols), then consumes only N symbols backwards.
; The low five bits of each symbol are shifted into a 21-byte chain at $08B9.
; $D1D0-$D1EF expands 19 raw bytes using bitmap bits from MSB downward,
; inserting zero for set bits and consuming $08BC onward for clear bits.
; Synthetic all-zero encoding length glyph $09 normalizes to $00 (not $40),
; so it fails the length gate; validator also explicitly rejects zero checksum.
; Native decoder has no upper-length/alphabet guard here and does not enforce
; canonical padding or bitmap population. Malformed input needs emulator tests;
; companion decode() deliberately adds bounds/alphabet/physical-length checks.
;
; P05 GLYPH TRANSLATION, PREFIX AND DISPLAY -- STATIC-DECODE
; $D980-$D9AE forward glyph mapping:
;   $40->$09, $41->$21, $45->$67, $49->$3F, $4F->$65, $55->$78.
; $D1A1-$D1CF reverse:
;   $09->$00, $21->$41, $67->$45, $3F->$49, $65->$4F, $78->$55.
; All other values unchanged. Five low bits recover symbols despite $09->$00.
; Screen verification establishes $3F='?', $67=double quote, $65=semicolon,
; $78=small hollow circle, $5B=minus, $5C=plus, $5D=triangle, $5E=square,
; $5F=filled circle, $02-$09=digits 2-9, $21=exclamation mark (see P10).
; Prefix selector $D896 uses $10D0 table at $03:D8B8:
;   01 07 03 02 02 06 0E 0E 09 0A
; First matching table index selects one of the first ten pairs at $D5B3:
; $01 RZ; $07 CB; $03 RG; $02 XJ; $06 GS; $0E KR; $09 NR; $0A FX.
; Duplicate $02/$0E entries choose the first match. This matters for old-code
; fallback: two prefix bytes alone do not uniquely represent a complete state.
; No match: each prefix glyph is glyph(($0465/$0466 & $1F) | $41).
; Source/purpose of $0465/$0466 remains UNKNOWN (do not label RNG yet).
; Generated render layout: two prefix glyphs, encoded-length glyph (inserted
; at $0885 after copying), N data glyphs with $6F separators after each group
; of six total characters. Formatting starts at $0851 with the first three
; data symbols before the first separator, and uses Y=3,10,17,...,52.
; End is $FA then $FF. Display copy $084E-$0880 -> $0883-$08B5 (51 bytes).
; The $D93C-$D94E alternative glyph transformation block has no incoming edge
; from the decoded generator path: $D93A skips it. No reachability claim beyond
; this path. Keep it preserved pending complete cross-reference inspection.
;
; P06 INPUT UI AND ERRORS -- STATIC-DECODE / PARTIAL RUNTIME CONFIRMATION
; Keyboard script $03:D3B6-$D415; lookup $03:D4B6-$D4E0 (43 bytes):
; 21 42 43 44 67 46 47 48 3F FC 4A 4B 4C 4D 4E 65 50 51 52 FD
