; Byte0 + $1080 gives the live-state address; byte1 is the attribute below.
; $02:D7B7-$D7C8 obtains a name ID from $02:DA3E. The name pointer directory
; at $01:B78D is 256 little-endian words, followed by $FF-terminated strings
; in bank $01. The catalog matches 34 equipment names against actual menu text.
; Recorded native spelling is preserved (ERRANDBOT, MILACLE KICK, REFLEXTAR).
; Directory bounds: $D946-$DA11 contains 102 records (indices $00-$65).
; $DA12 begins another table; $DA3E-$DAA3 contains 102 name IDs; $DAA4 begins
; enum pointers. Catalog emits 101 nonzero-index entries. $D67D permits item
; values <$70, which exceeds these physical table bounds: $66-$6F would read
; neighboring data. Do not label those bytes valid extra items or free space.
;
; Attributes (A/X/Y8 demonstrated by menu execution; reusable caller/DP/DBR
; contracts require their own patch entry audit): kind=attribute & 3;
; selector=(attribute & $7C)>>2; bit7 is a separate handling flag.
; kind0: quantity/multibyte scalar. $D6C3 chooses defaults via $DA12; $D7C9
; reads group+1 state bytes; selector's low3 select row and high2 select group.
; kind1: ownership bit. $D721 ANDs state byte with $DA36[selector & 7].
; kind2: exact selection. $D740 compares live byte to selector.
; kind3: indirect category selection. $D75A reads live byte, subtracts 1,
; selects pointer $DAA4[2*selector], reads that item's ID and re-enters $D67D.
; Getter $D67D returns item/status in DP $04, name ID in DP $0C/$0D and
; scratch DP $00-$0F; missing/invalid state can produce $FF. Not patch scratch.
; Grant/set $D7F2 dispatches by kind: scalar $D806; bit OR $D832;
; exact STA selector $D842; indirect increment live byte $D851.
; Remove/decrement entry $D863 has its own dispatch; do not assume OR/TSB
; semantics are interchangeable with removing an item or loading a saved value.
;
; Native scalar grant path $D806 calls $D8CB (limits via $DA24), then increments
; only if the getter returns negative/missing because current count is below
; limit. At/above limit it writes the old count without incrementing. This is
; not a universal clamp of arbitrarily injected high values. Relevant single-
; byte limit row at $DA2A: 1,8,50,255,3,3,15 (additional entries are other groups).
; Thus this standard grant path's caps are MEAT BUN=1, SWEET BUN=8,
; BONZEBOT=1, CUPSULE=8, BOO BOMB=8, DRAGON EGG=15. This resolves the earlier
; mismatch between UI's ability to display injected 15 and one-bit passwords.
; Other paths may write directly; retain the distinction from exhaustive limits.
