; $02:D8F7 tests, $D922 sets, $D933 clears an indexed flag using mask table
; $DA36: 01 02 04 08 10 20 40 80. $D912 computes byte index=floor(A/8),
; bit index=A&7. With A8, the addressable extent is $1800-$181F (256 flags).
; Callers $02:9F3F (test),$9F4F (set),$9F55 (clear) supply $17BF.
; Exact story/treasure meanings of individual flags remain unresolved.
; Normal payload saves only $1800 bit0; $D146 restores it via TSB, preserving
; all other bits. Checkpoint helper preloads $1800/$1801=01 A5 or FE A5 and
; loads the normal RZ code whose flag0 is zero. Both byte pairs survive exactly
; after world entry (flags_reload_* captures). In first case loaded packed
; state now differs from decoded payload at P[15] because flag0 remains one.
; Normal restore is therefore dependent on its initialized memory context; it
; is not a pure replacement of all persistent state. SRAM design must decide
; which of the indexed flags persist and initialize or overwrite owned flags
; deliberately, rather than calling unpack over arbitrary old live memory.
